## THE LINUX LEG (Tuesday's ledger rule of 2026-11-25: the RD-1037 cells passed on macOS and failed on the hosted Linux build because their premise was a path the product treats as persistent)
Which cell's premise depends on a platform path, env or file system? **(1) The route cells (T1 to T8)** build a real `JsonStorage` in an `os.tmpdir()` folder; the product logs "Persistence sentinel created on ephemeral path ... (expected for /tmp)" there (printed by the drafter's probe) and works; the form file `__tests__/entra-provisioning-setup.test.js` does the same and is in the hosted suite, so the premise is the same as a cell that already passes on the hosted runner, but this kit's cells are **UNMEASURED there**. **(2) The listener:** the route cells bind through `listenOnReservedPort` (the project helper; never `app.listen(0)`: its own comment cites RD-203) inside this worktree's port band (slots below). **(3) The SQLite cells (L1 to L6)** open a file under `os.tmpdir()` with the project's `sqlite3` module, as RD-973's and RD-913's cells do; the native binding's behaviour on the hosted Linux runner is exercised by those existing cells, not by these. **(4) The settings encryption** keys off the machine identity (`jsonStorage.getMachineIdentifier` prefers the env values `MACHINE_ID` and `WEBSITE_SITE_NAME` over a stored file, per RD-728's text); a cell here only round-trips a value inside one process, so a set env value cannot change its verdict, and **no cell reads or sets either variable.** **(5) Time:** no cell sleeps or compares two timestamps for inequality (a re-stamp assertion is NOT written; equality of an unchanged issue date is). **(6) `process.env`, `TMPDIR`, `PATH` and the working directory are not assigned by any cell.**
- **Measured before READY, by the Linux SHAPE (the instrument: `TMPDIR=/tmp` in the real environment plus `RUNNER_TEMP` a persistent dot-free folder; the shape that read B2099's two hosted reds):** (a) the two new files in the lane in the Linux shape and the own shape (`lane2139.sh`); (b) the thirty-three neighbour files in the Linux shape (the lane's last run); (c) the WHOLE suite in the Linux shape once (step 5, `linux-update`, the run that also writes the counts); (d) the plain verify in the own shape at K (the CI predicate with the committed counts). The own shape keeps the wrapper shell's `TMPDIR`; **never unset `TMPDIR` to make an "own environment" control** (on this machine `os.tmpdir()` answers `/tmp` when it is unset: that is a Linux shape).
