## THE PROPERTY (every behaviour: the cells that hold it, three shapes of its subject that the cells do NOT use, and each one covered or named)
**(A) RD-455.** *A scim-mode save that omits the bearer token (absent, null, empty or only spaces) succeeds when the stored mode is already scim and a token is stored, and leaves the stored token and its issue date exactly as they were (byte-equal); every submitted token is still validated, so a short or non-string token is refused whether or not one is stored; a save with no stored token and no submitted token is refused with the existing error; a switch into scim from another mode, a save after a revoke and a stale-version save behave as before; the audit row of a kept-token save says a token is configured and carries no token text.* **Cells (specified below):** V1 and V2 (the validator keeps), V3 (no keep without the flag, and the flag must be exactly `true`), V4 (a short token), V5 (non-string tokens), V6 (a valid token still replaces), T1 (the mapping-only save), T2 (the mapping removal), T3 (no stored token), T4 (a switch into scim), T5 (after a revoke), T6 (the audit row), T7 (a new token replaces; the same token again keeps its date), T8 (a stale version). **Shapes outside the tested one (the stored mode is scim, a 43-character token, one mapping), each stated:** (i) *a stored token whose value cannot be decrypted* (a key change): the route reads it through `getSetting` as the GET does; what that returns for an undecryptable blob is NOT tested, **named limit** (the keep then follows the GET's own predicate, and the GET would say not configured only if the read returns an empty value); (ii) *whitespace inside or around a real token*: the validator trims a submitted token as before; a spaces-only value is "omitted" and covered by V2; a token with inner spaces is not tested, **named limit**; (iii) *a concurrent save and a revoke*: both routes run inside the router's own exclusive section (`exclusive`, line 199 of the route at main, which the drafter did not change), so the flag is read and used under it; **no cell races them, named limit**. Further named limits: the SCIM endpoint's own use of the stored token is not read or changed; `mode: 'graph'` and `'off'` take the validator's other branches and are unchanged (no cell of this file, covered by the existing config cells in the neighbour list); the panel's text for a kept token (LEFT FOR OTHERS 2).
**(B) RD-981.** *A failed setQuickQuestions call sends no INSERT after the failing one and no INSERT is in flight when its ROLLBACK is requested; a good list is stored in order, the empty list commits the delete, a DELETE failure still rejects with the original error and a ROLLBACK, and a failed call leaves the table as it was; a call still rejects only after its ROLLBACK has finished (RD-973's property, untouched).* **Cells (specified below):** L1 (a 50-row list with a null first row: one INSERT sent), L2 (the late order modelled), L3 (a null at index 25: 26 INSERTs sent), L4 (a good 50-row list), L5 (the empty list), L6 (a DELETE failure). **Shapes outside the tested one (a 50-row list, a null as the failing row), each stated:** (i) *a failure that is not the NOT NULL constraint* (a disk error, a locked database on an INSERT): the same callback path; **no cell injects it, named limit**; (ii) *a list longer than 50 or a non-array argument*: not tested (a non-array throws inside the callback exactly as at main: **named limit, unchanged behaviour**); (iii) *the real race*: the cells count sends and model the order; **the rate of the real leak inside a jest worker is NOT measured (Q-5)**; one of the drafter's two plain-node trials at main's shape leaked a row (FOUND 2), which is an observation, not a rate. Further named limits: a COMMIT error still rejects without a ROLLBACK (as at main); the method's only callers outside tests are none by a text search (dynamic dispatch not searched).
**(C)** Nothing is built for RD-728, the panel's wording, an admin surface, the other store's method of the same name, or a trial loop. **Tested shapes:** the lane's runs (red-first at main in both shapes, green in both, each mutation red on at least its named cells), the whole suite once in the Linux shape, the plain verify. **Shapes outside, said so:** the production setting and a hosted instance; a real Linux file system and sqlite binding; the hosted build's real timing.

